@gate 3.0.0
# Pattern: the GateRegex ADMISSION BOUNDARY (GATEREGEX.md section 9).
#
# The security property this drawing proves:
#   No pattern can reach the matching operation unless an admitted compiler
#   produced the certificate consumed by that exact operation.
#
# It is proven by the TYPE, not by a convention. LiveRequest is
# construction: canonical-only and zone: semantic, and compile.certified is its
# ONLY producer - so a matcher reachable without a certificate is not merely
# discouraged, it is unrepresentable, and GATE-SEM-014 reports if a second
# producer is ever added.
#
# WHY THE SUBJECT GOES THROUGH THE COMPILER TOO, and this is the whole lesson:
# the first draft wired IN.subject straight to the matcher and kept only the
# certificate coming from the gate. GATE-SEM-014 refused it, correctly - the
# matcher was reachable from IN on a path that never passed the compiler, so the
# certificate was an INPUT IT RECEIVED rather than a route it had to take. That
# is the same fail-open the shipped v3 reference has in 03-database-query, and
# cookbook 7b names it: convert, do not bless. The compiler now takes both and
# emits ONE admitted request; there is exactly one route to the matcher.
#
# Note what is NOT drawn: the matcher's interior. .gate secures the perimeter of
# a component, never its interior - the NFA simulation, class membership and
# span handling stay the engine's own responsibility and its own tests'.
#
# Four outcomes stay four terminals. Collapsing pattern-refused into no-match is
# the fail-open GATEREGEX.md section 4 names: one says this input was rejected,
# the other says NO INPUT ON THIS PATH HAS BEEN CHECKED SINCE DEPLOY.
CIRCUIT admit_and_match(pattern: RawPattern, subject: RawText) -> MatchedText
  INTENT "A pattern may only be matched with a certificate its own compilation produced."
  REQUIRES:
    effect compute.cpu
  PARTS:
    [compile :: re.compile@1.0.0 dialect=bounded]
    [run :: re.match@1.0.0]
  WIRES:
    IN.pattern -> compile.pattern
    IN.subject -> compile.subject
    compile.veto -> DENY.pattern_refused
    compile.unavailable -> DENY.compiler_unavailable
    compile.certified -> run.request
    run.match -> OUT.value
    run.no -> DENY.no_match
    run.undecided -> DENY.undecided
END
